OWNER DECISION, 2026-08-30. Approved in session; this reverses a reversal, so
both directions are recorded rather than one overwriting the other.

WHY THE GATE WAS IN THE PER-PR PIPELINE (INV-bobor). It used to run only on the
cron. A self-claim regression -- runtime-cli-no-host-fs going
confirmed_with_caveats -> violated -- sat on green dev for 63 COMMITS. Detection
was never the failure: the cron arm named it exactly, at the 2026-08-19 01:00
firing. DELIVERY failed, three ways: MASKING (full-suite publishes ONE aggregate
status for six steps, and at the 2026-08-20 firing that status was `failure`
while this gate PASSED -- the red came from a flaky tracker TUI test);
ADDRESSING (a cron status lands on a non-HEAD commit, and ci-debug rendered only
HEAD); and ERROR-IS-NOT-FAILURE (a pipeline dying inside the gate is
indistinguishable from one whose gate passed).

WHY IT LEAVES NOW. The move into the per-PR pipeline was justified on a measured
price: 3m49s wall-clock, running CONCURRENTLY with a 935s pytest step -- i.e.
free. That number has gone stale by ~3.7x. The gate now takes ~14 minutes and
finishes LAST, so it is the critical path of every code PR.

--minimal, MEASURED COLD, TAKES 30.4% OFF WHAT REMAINS:

    default   1450s (24.2 min)
    --minimal 1009s (16.8 min)     -441s

Both arms confirmed genuinely cold by their "No cached results found" line, run
against separate throwaway XDG_CACHE_HOME dirs, and they produced BYTE-IDENTICAL
output (309,026 bytes, 18/18 verdicts equal). It declines side outputs the gate
never reads -- budget-tier previews, per-handler slices, sketch pre-computation
-- not analysis scope, so the saving costs no coverage.

THE FIRST MEASUREMENT SAID 24x AND WAS WRONG. Two worktrees at the same commit
share a survey cache: it keys on repo CONTENT, not path, so the second arm rode
the first's survey and 33s was a cache hit. Only the first arm's stderr carried
the cold-cache line. Anyone re-measuring must ASSERT that line, not assume it;
the probe in scripts' history does.

ONE OF THE THREE DELIVERY FAILURES IS FIXED; TWO ARE ACCEPTED, NOT SOLVED.
`ci-debug cron-status` (INV-bozid remedy (b), landed earlier today) closes
ADDRESSING. MASKING and ERROR-IS-NOT-FAILURE remain, because they follow from
the single aggregate status: a drift verdict now reads as "full-suite is red"
and needs a log fetch to attribute. That is cheap WHILE THE AGGREGATE IS USUALLY
GREEN. The failure mode is a sibling going chronically red, at which point the
signal carries no information and nobody reads the log -- which is exactly what
produced the 63 commits. The risk is lower than in August (the flaky tracker TUI
test behind the 2026-08-20 masking was fixed the same day as this change) but it
is not gone.

TRIPWIRE, recorded in four places so it is acted on rather than rediscovered
(woodpecker.yml tombstone, full-suite.yml header, the test docstring, and the
ci-debug protocol playbook): if full-suite starts sitting red, give this gate its
own workflow file and therefore its own commit-status context -- INV-bozid
remedy (a), verified viable, and it must reuse the EXISTING `full-suite` cron
name because crons are configured in repo settings and not in these files.

THE PIN IS REWRITTEN, NOT DELETED. test_ci_self_claims_gate_scope.py had three
assertions about the per-PR arm; it now pins what remains -- the cron arm exists,
stays unconditional, and ACTUALLY INVOKES the script (the first two both pass for
a step whose commands install dependencies and stop). Its docstring carries both
decisions. NO TEST ASSERTS THE PER-PR ARM'S ABSENCE: pinning a removal would
block whoever re-adds it, and INV-bobor says a future measurement may justify
exactly that.

Two comments that became false are corrected in the same change: full-suite.yml
deferred to "the per-PR arm ... which carries the path filter and the full
rationale", and the protocol playbook's topology section described ci.yml as
gating merge without noting the gate had left it.

smart-test 84 passed, mypy ratchet clean.

